the opioid crisis in America is quite  severe in 2018 10.3 million people ages  12 and older misused opioids most of  these people misuse prescription  painkillers often turning to the cheaper  more addictive alternative heroin there  are two types of opioids endogenous and  exogenes endogenius opioids are  neurotransmitters created and regulated  by the body exogenes opioids or drugs  derived from plants and produced  synthetically these drugs are the source  of the modern opioid crisis both  endogenes and exogenes opioids interact  with opioid receptors these receptors  are coupled with G proteins which help  modulate neuron signaling one way that  the use of opioids can affect neuron  Pathways is by preventing  neurotransmitter release here's how it  works in the presynaptic neuron when the  opioid drugs bind to an opioid receptor  the g-protein complex splits into two  subunits G Alpha and G beta gamma the G  beta gamma subunit binds to the  voltage-gated calcium channel making  them unable to open and blocking calcium  ions from entering neurotransmitter  release to the next neuron is unable to  occur because it depends on the presence  of calcium ions in the presynaptic  neuron exogeneous opioids can be  extremely dangerous because unlike  endogeneous opioids they are not  moderated and bind to all available  receptor sites blocking crucial Pathways  between the brain and respiratory system  every day an average of 130 Americans  die from opioid overdoses  now it's known as a chemical compound  that can be used to block the effects of  opioids saving lives in overdose  situations it has a similar structure to  the opioids and binds to the same  receptor site without activating the G  protein this prevents opioids from  binding and keeps the neuron pathway  between the brain and respiratory system  open ensuring that people can still  breathe the misuse of opioids can ruin  lives some solutions help fight this  crisis are to be well educated in the  effects to not misuse prescription  opioids and to seek professional help we  hope that if you or a loved one has been  impacted by the opioid crisis you feel  comfortable sharing your story and  asking for help when needed and remember  avoid the opioid
 

